Like, how can you
live with yourself?

00:35:43.574 --> 00:35:44.509
I mean that,

00:35:44.509 --> 00:35:46.010
that is so immoral.

00:35:46.010 --> 00:35:48.579
- So what do you think the
answer to that question is?

00:35:48.579 --> 00:35:49.780
How do they,

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:51.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you have to cover
some idiotic thing

00:35:51.516 --> 00:35:53.784
that Stelter said or Cuomo,

00:35:53.784 --> 00:35:57.088
just these clown people, when
you have to cover it, right?

00:35:57.088 --> 00:35:58.990
Or Don Lumon as you call them,

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:01.159
like, how do you think
they live with themselves

00:36:01.159 --> 00:36:03.161
at this point when they
just lie again and again,

00:36:03.161 --> 00:36:05.496 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we have the internet
to expose the lies.

00:36:05.496 --> 00:36:08.466
This isn't 20 years ago
when you were on CNN,

00:36:08.466 --> 00:36:09.700
and we couldn't expose things,

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:12.370
we can expose it now
and they still do it.

00:36:12.370 --> 00:36:15.673
- Well, it's, I guess I
would ask myself, like,

00:36:15.673 --> 00:36:19.477
I mean, I lie if I'm really
cornered or something, I lie.

00:36:19.477 --> 00:36:21.979
I really try not to.

00:36:21.979 --> 00:36:23.347
I try never to lie on TV.

00:36:23.347 --> 00:36:25.149 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just don't, you know,
I don't like lying.

00:36:25.149 --> 00:36:28.986
I certainly do it, you know,
out of weakness or whatever,

00:36:28.986 --> 00:36:32.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
but to systematically
lie like that

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:36.160
without asking yourself,
like, why am I doing this?

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:37.728
So if these people ask them,

00:36:37.728 --> 00:36:38.896
just why am I doing this?

00:36:38.896 --> 00:36:41.399 align:start size:88% position:13%
They'll say well,
because I wanna protect

00:36:41.399 --> 00:36:43.701 align:start size:97% position:3%
the system, because I really
believe in the system.

00:36:43.701 --> 00:36:45.770
Okay, who's running the system?

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:49.574
You're aligned to
defend Jeff Bezos.

00:36:49.574 --> 00:36:52.310
Like, you're treating Bill Gates

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:55.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
like some sort of moral
leader, like, are you kidding?

00:36:55.079 --> 00:36:57.381
Like, how dare you do that?

00:36:57.381 --> 00:37:00.518
How dare you use your power

00:37:00.518 --> 00:37:05.156 align:start size:88% position:13%
to protect and guard the
powerful even as you,

00:37:06.591 --> 00:37:09.660 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, put your boot on the
neck of the weakest people,

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:12.763
some Catholic school kid from
Kentucky, it's like a parody.

00:37:12.763 --> 00:37:13.598
Are you kidding?

00:37:13.598 --> 00:37:15.733
The kid's a child,

00:37:15.733 --> 00:37:19.270
and you're using your
power to crush him,

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:20.571
to wreck his life,

00:37:20.571 --> 00:37:22.473
like, that shocks me.

00:37:22.473 --> 00:37:23.708
And I have to say,

00:37:23.708 --> 00:37:25.142
there've been many times

00:37:25.142 --> 00:37:28.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the 25 years I've
been in TV where I think,

00:37:28.045 --> 00:37:30.481
you know, are we using this,

00:37:30.481 --> 00:37:33.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
like, very substantial
power that we have,

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:38.155 align:start size:97% position:3%
to put pictures on the
screen to hurt weak people?

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:40.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I have done that
inadvertently over the years

00:37:40.958 --> 00:37:44.428
because I got carried away,
but I really try not to.

00:37:44.428 --> 00:37:47.999
And everyone who works
on our show is very aware

00:37:47.999 --> 00:37:53.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
of like the most basic rule,
which is don't piss down,

00:37:53.871 --> 00:37:55.139
don't attack people beneath you.

00:37:55.139 --> 00:37:56.741
If you're gonna, you
know, take a punch,

00:37:56.741 --> 00:37:58.142
make sure it's upward.

00:37:58.142 --> 00:38:00.177 align:start size:88% position:13%
Someone who's richer,
stronger, more powerful,

00:38:00.177 --> 00:38:03.381
in charge of more things
than you were, punch up.

00:38:03.381 --> 00:38:05.349
Like, that's just
like a life rule.

00:38:05.349 --> 00:38:07.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
And people who punched
down are the worst,

00:38:07.852 --> 00:38:10.554
they should have no power
whatsoever, in my opinion.

00:38:10.554 --> 00:38:11.722
- Right.

00:38:11.722 --> 00:38:12.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the irony is
they're punching down

00:38:12.923 --> 00:38:15.293 align:start size:88% position:13%
while pretending they're
doing the opposite,

00:38:15.293 --> 00:38:16.527
which is the grossest part.

00:38:16.527 --> 00:38:17.795
All right, wait, let's
talk about the book,

00:38:17.795 --> 00:38:19.196 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause I thought it
was really interesting.

00:38:19.196 --> 00:38:21.799
So the book is basically
a collection of essays

00:38:21.799 --> 00:38:23.734
that you've written
over a couple decades

00:38:23.734 --> 00:38:25.102
over about 30 years.

00:38:25.102 --> 00:38:27.571
And there's a line
right in the front

00:38:27.571 --> 00:38:29.674 align:start size:81% position:19%
that I thought was
great actually.

00:38:29.674 --> 00:38:33.444 align:start size:97% position:3%
You say magazine journalism
is worth remembering.

00:38:33.444 --> 00:38:35.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that you also then
go on to say that,

00:38:35.079 --> 00:38:37.315
you know, some of the stuff
in these pages you got right,

00:38:37.315 --> 00:38:38.516
some of the stuff you got wrong,

00:38:38.516 --> 00:38:40.017
some of it could have
been written today

00:38:40.017 --> 00:38:42.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
and would be as
important, some of it not,

00:38:42.186 --> 00:38:44.889
but magazine journalism
is worth remembering.

00:38:44.889 --> 00:38:46.524
I thought that was
a key line there,

00:38:46.524 --> 00:38:49.327 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it seems
really dead right now.

00:38:49.327 --> 00:38:51.095
Like, when I read
any of the things

00:38:51.095 --> 00:38:53.297
that 10 years ago
were respectable,

00:38:53.297 --> 00:38:55.032
The Atlantic, just
any of the things,

00:38:55.032 --> 00:38:58.836
like they're just
all terrible now.

00:38:58.836 --> 00:39:01.038
Was there a moment
that it shifted?

00:39:01.038 --> 00:39:03.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was it as pure as maybe
we all thought it will be,

00:39:03.441 --> 00:39:06.077
was at one point
or, or any of that?

00:39:06.077 --> 00:39:07.311
- Well, the funny thing is this,

00:39:07.311 --> 00:39:09.613
I grew up thinking it
was kind of shallow.

00:39:10.815 --> 00:39:11.982
My father was (indistinct).

00:39:11.982 --> 00:39:13.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, God, were
really in trouble now.

00:39:13.484 --> 00:39:14.885
- I know.

00:39:14.885 --> 00:39:16.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
So my father was a
magazine writer, actually,

00:39:16.854 --> 00:39:17.688
when I was born

00:39:19.123 --> 00:39:20.658
and a successful one.

00:39:20.658 --> 00:39:23.160
And I remember him saying to
me in this sort of acid way

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:25.796
about a relative of ours who
was smart, and he said, oh,

00:39:25.796 --> 00:39:27.164
that person is smart.

00:39:27.164 --> 00:39:29.266
And my father goes, clever,

00:39:29.266 --> 00:39:30.735
she reads magazines.

00:39:32.002 --> 00:39:33.838
I'll never forget that,

00:39:33.838 --> 00:39:35.840 align:start size:88% position:13%
She reads magazines.
- The lowest of the low.

00:39:35.840 --> 00:39:37.341
Right, rather than books.

00:39:37.341 --> 00:39:39.510 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like smart people,
deep people read books.

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:41.245 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because as my father
always told us,

00:39:41.245 --> 00:39:44.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
there is value in the
process of reading a book,

00:39:44.081 --> 00:39:46.484 align:start size:88% position:13%
you have to follow a
narrative for 300 pages.

00:39:46.484 --> 00:39:49.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have to pay attention
to the same storyline

00:39:49.186 --> 00:39:50.888
as it unfolds and develops,

00:39:50.888 --> 00:39:52.656
and that's really good
training for your brain,

00:39:52.656 --> 00:39:55.059 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause you see things
longitudinally.

00:39:55.059 --> 00:39:56.460 align:start size:81% position:19%
And you understand
that sometimes

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:57.962
the consequences of decisions

00:39:57.962 --> 00:40:00.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
aren't apparent for a very
long time, like that's,

00:40:00.197 --> 00:40:02.066
you know, long stories are good.

00:40:02.066 --> 00:40:05.569 align:start size:97% position:3%
But you know, I was a magazine
writer and a book author,

00:40:05.569 --> 00:40:07.138
but really a magazine writer.

00:40:07.138 --> 00:40:09.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
And now that doesn't
exist really,

00:40:09.607 --> 00:40:13.511
and the reason it doesn't exist
is because people can't read

00:40:13.511 --> 00:40:14.979
for 30 minutes at a time,

00:40:14.979 --> 00:40:16.213
because their brains

00:40:16.213 --> 00:40:18.816
have been so totally
changed by the iPhone

00:40:18.816 --> 00:40:20.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they don't even have
the ability to do it.

00:40:20.818 --> 00:40:22.987
It's so amazing to me.

00:40:22.987 --> 00:40:24.622
So that's part of
what's going on

00:40:24.622 --> 00:40:28.325
is the process of staring
at this tiny screen for

00:40:28.325 --> 00:40:30.327
half your day or more

00:40:30.327 --> 00:40:35.332
has changed the way you
metabolize information.

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:37.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I remember when
Twitter first came out,

00:40:37.768 --> 00:40:39.904
I made fun of it to
everybody I knew,

00:40:39.904 --> 00:40:42.473 align:start size:88% position:13%
like 140 or whoever
number characters, like,

00:40:42.473 --> 00:40:45.309 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's the space with which
we have to express our

00:40:45.309 --> 00:40:47.678
the deepest truths in
American society, like,

00:40:47.678 --> 00:40:48.913
we're kind of, actually.

00:40:50.147 --> 00:40:51.415
If that's, you know,

00:40:51.415 --> 00:40:52.983
'cause the medium
really matters.

00:40:54.185 --> 00:40:56.587
And now it's like
totally normal.

00:40:56.587 --> 00:40:58.055 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, the
president United States

00:40:58.055 --> 00:41:00.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
was communicating his
thoughts on Twitter and now,

00:41:00.257 --> 00:41:01.992
you know, that's the forum.

00:41:01.992 --> 00:41:03.961
And so it's the medium itself,

00:41:03.961 --> 00:41:06.864
it's not simply that the
business model went away,

00:41:06.864 --> 00:41:08.299
and that's all true, of course,

00:41:08.299 --> 00:41:11.001 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was taken over by the
worst people, yeah, obviously.

00:41:11.001 --> 00:41:13.604
But it was that the
American population

00:41:13.604 --> 00:41:17.341
became incapable of
consuming this media

00:41:17.341 --> 00:41:18.776
because it was too long.

00:41:19.977 --> 00:41:22.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
Oh, that's so awful
if you think about it.

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:25.716 align:start position:0%
- No, listen as a guy that just
took a month off the phone,

00:41:25.716 --> 00:41:27.284
I get it.

00:41:27.284 --> 00:41:29.119
I'm more patient now.

00:41:29.119 --> 00:41:31.055
Even when I opened
up Twitter again,

00:41:31.055 --> 00:41:34.658
on a quick scroll, I was like,
these people are all insane.

00:41:34.658 --> 00:41:36.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
Even though it had nothing
to do whether I agreed

00:41:36.827 --> 00:41:38.229
or disagreed, it was just like

00:41:38.229 --> 00:41:40.498
this short burst thought war

00:41:40.498 --> 00:41:43.067
is just making us all crazy.

00:41:43.067 --> 00:41:44.635
- I know.

00:41:44.635 --> 00:41:46.270
And you know, that the other
thing is it's all transmit,

00:41:46.270 --> 00:41:47.538
that's the problem with it.

00:41:47.538 --> 00:41:50.207
And for me, that's the
thing that I miss most,

00:41:50.207 --> 00:41:52.176
especially now that
I'm a talk show host,

00:41:52.176 --> 00:41:55.446 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I don't do
any real reporting

00:41:56.514 --> 00:41:58.015
of the kind that I used to do,

00:41:58.015 --> 00:41:59.483
is that all I do is talk,

00:41:59.483 --> 00:42:02.086
all I do is express my views.

00:42:02.086 --> 00:42:04.922
And that imperils
your soul after

00:42:04.922 --> 00:42:06.590
a pretty short period of time.

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:07.791
Like, it's not good for you.

00:42:07.791 --> 00:42:08.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you know,
any talk show hosts,

00:42:08.993 --> 00:42:10.861
like they're unbearable.

00:42:10.861 --> 00:42:12.029
You know, I can think of

00:42:12.029 --> 00:42:13.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
a couple in particular,
- I'm trying, man.

00:42:13.564 --> 00:42:16.400
you just cannot talk to them,

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:19.303 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they don't
listen, they only talk,

00:42:19.303 --> 00:42:21.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're only on
transmit, no receive.

00:42:21.872 --> 00:42:24.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the beauty of writing
magazine stories was

00:42:24.508 --> 00:42:27.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
part of the deal, in fact,
the essence of the deal

00:42:27.011 --> 00:42:29.647 align:start size:97% position:3%
was going out and listening
to people shutting up,

00:42:29.647 --> 00:42:31.715 line:0%
blending in, watching,

00:42:31.715 --> 00:42:34.051 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
taking in other
people's experiences

00:42:34.051 --> 00:42:35.920 line:0%
and then thinking about them.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:37.855 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And you have to have
that in your life

00:42:37.855 --> 00:42:39.089
or else you'd just become,

00:42:39.089 --> 00:42:41.091
and men as they age,
by the way, become

00:42:41.091 --> 00:42:44.028
less capable of listening

00:42:44.028 --> 00:42:47.031
and more insistent on
talking all the time.

00:42:47.031 --> 00:42:48.299
And I'm already that way anyway.

00:42:48.299 --> 00:42:50.000
I mean, I just, I can't shut up.

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:53.671
So for me, it's really, the
job is really dangerous.

00:42:53.671 --> 00:42:55.306
Like, I could wind up,

00:42:55.306 --> 00:42:56.273
and I've told my wife,

00:42:56.273 --> 00:42:57.875
I keep a 38 next to my bed,

00:42:57.875 --> 00:42:59.376
my whole life I was
a little, you know,

00:42:59.376 --> 00:43:02.046 align:start size:88% position:13%
two inch barrel,
special and I was like,

00:43:02.046 --> 00:43:05.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
just take it headshot,
end it, if I become

00:43:05.382 --> 00:43:09.186
like a lot of these people
we both know in our business,

00:43:09.186 --> 00:43:11.956
just end it before I humiliate
myself in front of the kids,

00:43:11.956 --> 00:43:12.890
before they don't wanna,

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:14.158
you know, be around me anymore

00:43:14.158 --> 00:43:16.727
'cause I can't shut up
and listen, you know?

00:43:16.727 --> 00:43:18.228
So that's, I do miss that,

00:43:18.228 --> 00:43:20.631
I really, really miss that,
just going out and listening.

00:43:20.631 --> 00:43:22.166
- So you also lead in the book,

00:43:22.166 --> 00:43:24.401
you talk a little bit about
how the business has changed,

00:43:24.401 --> 00:43:26.170 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause it's not just
our brains have changed.

00:43:26.170 --> 00:43:29.607
So that's changed, you know,
sort of long form journalism,

00:43:29.607 --> 00:43:31.976 align:start position:0%
but also the way the structures
themselves has changed.

00:43:31.976 --> 00:43:34.612
And you juxtapose the way

00:43:34.612 --> 00:43:38.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
the publishing world
treated Josh Hawley's book

00:43:38.048 --> 00:43:39.850
versus Hunter Biden's book.

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:41.385
And I think it's
a perfect example

00:43:41.385 --> 00:43:44.588
of almost everything that's
wrong with things right now.

00:43:44.588 --> 00:43:46.056
- Yeah, it was so
distressing for me.

00:43:46.056 --> 00:43:48.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, the book is published
by Simon &amp; Schuster,

00:43:48.025 --> 00:43:50.427
it's the second book in a
row I've written for them,

00:43:50.427 --> 00:43:52.162 align:start size:84% position:16%
the last I would
ever write for them.

00:43:52.162 --> 00:43:53.998
I mean, it's such a
disgusting company

00:43:53.998 --> 00:43:55.232
run by disgusting people,

00:43:55.232 --> 00:43:56.967
but I didn't fully appreciate

00:43:56.967 --> 00:43:58.535
that I knew that,

00:43:58.535 --> 00:44:00.938
I knew that the guy who runs
it, this guy, John Karp was

00:44:00.938 --> 00:44:03.474
dishonest and not super smart.

00:44:03.474 --> 00:44:06.176
But you know, that goes for
a lot of people in our world.

00:44:06.176 --> 00:44:07.711
In January...

00:44:07.711 --> 00:44:09.680
- How do you really feel,
Tucker, come on, open up.

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:10.714
- Oh, he's awful,

00:44:10.714 --> 00:44:12.416
He's absolutely awful.

00:44:12.416 --> 00:44:14.418 align:start position:0%
But I know, you know, whatever,
you deal with awful people,

00:44:14.418 --> 00:44:16.754
I mean, you know, I can
be awful, so I get it.

00:44:16.754 --> 00:44:19.623
I'm not that judgy
in my personal life,

00:44:19.623 --> 00:44:23.193 align:start size:97% position:3%
but in January, right after,
you know, January 7th,

00:44:23.193 --> 00:44:24.695
Simon Schuster issues
a statement saying

00:44:24.695 --> 00:44:27.398
we're canceling Josh
Hawley's book because

00:44:27.398 --> 00:44:28.465
he is an insurrectionist.

00:44:28.465 --> 00:44:30.300
So I was like, hmm, Josh Hawley

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:31.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
seems kind of mild-mannered,
so I looked into it,

00:44:31.969 --> 00:44:34.371
and he basically, he
voted to, you know,

00:44:34.371 --> 00:44:36.340 align:start size:88% position:13%
stop the certification,
the vote,

00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:39.043 align:start size:88% position:13%
let's figure out the
vote was fair, whatever.

00:44:39.043 --> 00:44:40.344
Okay, and you can
agree or disagree,

00:44:40.344 --> 00:44:41.912
I wasn't calling for
anyone to do that.

00:44:41.912 --> 00:44:43.781
In point of fact,
just so you know,

00:44:43.781 --> 00:44:46.116
like, I'm not even
sure I was bothered,

00:44:46.116 --> 00:44:47.651
but he did it.

00:44:47.651 --> 00:44:50.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
Democrats did the same thing
after the 2016 election,

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:53.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
after 2004, like I watched
it, like, I know this.

00:44:53.891 --> 00:44:55.793
So I'm like, what made
Josh Hawley special?

00:44:55.793 --> 00:44:57.327
Nothing really,

00:44:57.327 --> 00:44:59.663
Democrats were mad at Josh
Hawley so Simon &amp; Schuster

